DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain tracks the intellectual trajectory of Enrique Dussel's
critique of Eurocentrism across three principal career phases:
(1) the early Latin American philosophy of liberation (1970s),
rooted in Levinasian ethics of alterity and a phenomenology of
the periphery; (2) the Apel-Habermas dialogue of the 1990s, in
which Dussel reformulated his ethics through engagement with
discourse ethics, transcendental pragmatics, and critical theory;
and (3) the late "Politics of Liberation" and Ethics of Liberation
in the Age of Globalization and Exclusion (1998/2013), where the
critique of Eurocentrism is recast historically through a
world-systems frame and the concept of transmodernity.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
These sources collectively allow tracing how Dussel's anti-
Eurocentric arguments shifted from an ethics of alterity to a
materialist/historical world-systems critique, and finally to a
pluriversal politics. The Apel debate volumes are essential for
understanding the inflection between phases two and three.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
English-language scholarship on Dussel's earliest Argentine-period
writings (Para una etica de la liberacion latinoamericana, 1973)
remains thin; most cited treatments are reviews or secondary
overviews rather than archival reconstructions.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Use Pimenta (2025) and Pachon Soto (2012) as scaffolding for
periodization; rely on Dussel (1997, 2007, 2013) for primary-text
self-narration of the trajectory; situate the Apel encounter via
Underside of Modernity (1996) and Barber's chapter on Apel.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Periodization/genealogy: 4 papers - reconstruct phase shifts
- Apel/discourse-ethics phase: 3 papers - dialogue with critical theory
- Levinasian/early phase: 3 papers - ethics of alterity foundations
- Late politics of liberation: 3 papers - transmodernity/world-system
====================================================================
}

